WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.531
Cloud security is the practice of protecting cloud-hosted systems, data, identities, networks, services, and workloads.

2
00:00:06.651 --> 00:00:19.712
It is not one product, one setting, or one team. It is an operating model for using elastic, API-driven infrastructure without losing control of access, exposure, data handling, and operational visibility.

3
00:00:19.832 --> 00:00:26.362
Cloud environments feel different from traditional on-premises environments because change is faster and more programmable.

4
00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:38.319
An engineer can create a resource, attach storage, grant a role, expose a service, or deploy a workload through an API, a pipeline, or an infrastructure as code change.

5
00:00:38.439 --> 00:00:43.337
That speed is useful, but it also means mistakes can scale quickly.

6
00:00:43.457 --> 00:00:50.804
A permissive policy can apply to many resources. A public storage bucket can expose more data than intended.

7
00:00:50.924 --> 00:00:57.863
A default network rule can make a service reachable before anyone has reviewed whether it should be.

8
00:00:57.983 --> 00:01:02.881
The core domains are identity, network, data, workloads, logging, configuration, and governance.

9
00:01:03.001 --> 00:01:10.756
Identity controls who and what can use the cloud APIs. Network controls decide what is reachable and from where.

10
00:01:10.876 --> 00:01:19.447
Data controls protect storage, databases, backups, keys, and retention. Workload controls cover virtual machines, containers, functions, images, secrets, and runtime behavior.

11
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:26.098
Logging and monitoring help teams understand who did what, from where, to which resource, and when.

12
00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:31.524
Configuration and governance make secure behavior repeatable across accounts, subscriptions, projects, and teams.

13
00:01:31.644 --> 00:01:35.726
A practical cloud security routine begins with inventory. What exists?

14
00:01:35.846 --> 00:01:39.111
Who owns it? What data does it handle?

15
00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:45.762
Which identities can change it? Is it public, private, or reachable through a controlled entry point?

16
00:01:45.882 --> 00:01:49.963
What logs are enabled? What baseline should this resource follow?

17
00:01:50.083 --> 00:01:55.389
These questions sound simple, but they are the foundation for every meaningful control.

18
00:01:55.509 --> 00:02:03.264
Cloud security also requires shared accountability. Developers, engineers, administrators, analysts, managers, and governance teams all touch the risk picture.

19
00:02:03.384 --> 00:02:15.221
The goal is not to slow every change to a crawl. The goal is to make secure defaults, least privilege, review, monitoring, and remediation part of normal engineering work.

20
00:02:15.341 --> 00:02:27.178
When teams know their assets, limit access, protect data, monitor activity, and review configuration continuously, cloud security becomes a repeatable way of operating rather than a last-minute checklist.
